#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379305 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4241972f-4023-5638-aa7a-9e4a75167c3d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Laplace Transform of an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "318b1fc5-c8ac-5222-97c4-516823c1b3e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379307} {:answer/id #uuid "480c8123-37fa-5b01-9148-d3afac4a6f39" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379308} {:answer/id #uuid "ebeeeade-5a9a-50d6-8b3f-06bdeba2eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379309} {:answer/id #uuid "b5a4f89e-487b-5285-81d0-8b9dc352381c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{s}{s+3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379310} {:answer/id #uuid "99a5d590-73a1-5fee-8891-d145e859422b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379311}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebeeeade-5a9a-50d6-8b3f-06bdeba2eddd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{s(s+3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379309} :answer-field/id #uuid "274f7524-cbad-52f6-b50b-80576d79abf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379306}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5a6c667-8ec3-50a3-8a5a-a2ecda7f0e19" :question/math-academy-id "q-330826" :question/problem "Given that $L{{e}^{at}}=\\frac{1}{s-a}$ for $s>a$, find the Laplace transform of\n$g(t)={\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{-3τ}\\,\\text{d}τ$\nfor $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluate the integral first:\n\n$$g(t)=\\int_0^t e^{-3\\tau}\\,d\\tau=\\frac{1-e^{-3t}}3.$$\n\nFor $s>0$, both exponential integrals converge, and\n\n$$\\mathcal L\\{g\\}(s)=\\frac13\\left(\\frac1s-\\frac1{s+3}\\right)=\\frac1{s(s+3)}.$$\n\nThis is option a. The integrand $g(t)$ tends to $1/3$. In fact, for $t\\ge1$, $g(t)\\ge(1-e^{-3})/3>0$, so the defining Laplace integral diverges for every real $s\\le0$. Thus the maximal real convergence domain is $s>0$. The original source's $s>-3$ is the convergence condition for $e^{-3t}$, not for its integral; extending the rational expression to negative $s$ does not extend the transform."}]}
